\description{
Combines one block of event rates per outcome x exposure (from
\code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}}, or from Stata's \code{strate}) into one table: the outcomes as
column groups -- Events, Person-Years (PY), and the rate with its
confidence interval, plus an incidence rate ratio column with
\code{rateratio} -- and the exposure groups as row blocks, each category
indented under its exposure label. R implementation of the Stata
\code{stratetab} command; the table prints, and is written to Excel with the
tabtools house style, and to CSV and Markdown, like the other commands.
}
\section{Blocks}{

\code{x} lists the blocks in Stata's \code{using()} order: every outcome for
exposure 1, then every outcome for exposure 2, and so on (with 3 outcomes
and 2 exposures: \verb{O1_E1, O2_E1, O3_E1, O1_E2, O2_E2, O3_E2}). Each block
is one of
\itemize{
\item a \code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} result (computed from time and event data): its level
is known;
\item a data frame in strate shape (\verb{_D}/\code{D}, \verb{_Y}/\code{Y}, \verb{_Rate}/\code{Rate},
\verb{_Lower}/\code{Lower}, \verb{_Upper}/\code{Upper}, plus a category column), such as a
\code{strate} file read with \code{haven::read_dta()};
\item the path of a \code{strate} output file (\code{.dta}, added when missing; needs
the haven package).
}

The category column is the first column that is not one of the five;
a block without one is a single row labelled \code{Overall}. A category
column named \code{D}, \code{Y}, \code{Rate}, \code{Lower} or \code{Upper} beside strate's \verb{_D},
\verb{_Y}, ... (a \code{strate} file whose grouping variable has one of those
names) is refused with a rename hint: R's blocks use those names for the
statistics (Stata's \code{stratetab} reads such a file). Categories show
their labels (factor levels, or \code{haven::labelled()} value labels, as
Stata's \code{decode}), character values as they are, and unlabelled numbers
as Stata's \verb{\%21.0g} (\code{1}, \code{10000000}); a blank or duplicated label is an
error. Within an exposure, every outcome's block must hold the same
categories: later outcomes are aligned to the first outcome's order by
label.
}

\section{Scaling and intervals}{

The table shows each block's events, its person-time divided by
\code{pyscale}, and its rate and bounds multiplied by \code{ratescale}, as they are
stored in the block: a block computed with \code{tt_rates(per = 1000)} (or
\verb{strate, per(1000)}) already holds rates per 1,000 time units and
person-time in thousands, so pass \code{ratescale = 1} and \code{pyscale = 1/1000}
for it. R knows the \code{per} of a \code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} result, so such blocks are
refused unless \code{ratescale} is given (the default would show rates \code{per}
times too high), blocks with different \code{per} are refused, and when
\code{ratescale} is given but a numeric \code{unitlabel} matches neither
\code{per * ratescale} nor \code{per * ratescale / pyscale} there is a warning
(\code{per = 1000} with \code{ratescale = 1000} is per million). A
\code{strate} file does not record its \code{per()}, so for one it is up to you. A block cannot say which time unit it counts in, so \code{unitlabel}
(the \verb{1,000} of the header \verb{Per 1,000 PY}) is yours to keep in step with
\code{ratescale}; person-time in days, for example, needs \code{pyscale = 365.25}
and \code{ratescale = 365250} for rates per 1,000 person-years.

The interval level is the blocks' own: a \code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} result records
it, and a strate-shaped block names it in the \code{"label"} attributes of
its interval columns (\code{strate} labels them \verb{Lower 95\% confidence limit}). All blocks must agree, and \code{level}, if given, must match them.
When any block carries no level, \code{level} is required: the level is
never assumed to be 95\%. The level labels the headers and sets the
rate-ratio intervals.

With \code{rateratio = TRUE}, each category of exposure 2, 3, ... is compared
with the category of the same label in exposure 1 (the reference group,
shown as \code{Ref.}): the rate ratio of the two rates with the log-normal
interval \verb{exp(log(IRR) -/+ z * sqrt(1/D1 + 1/D2))}, which assumes
independent, non-overlapping rates. A ratio without events on either
side (or a zero reference rate) is shown as a dash. Categories that do
not match exposure 1 one to one are an error. A rate without bounds
(\code{strate} gives none for zero events) is shown with a dash for its
interval (\code{0.0} and an en dash in parentheses), as in Stata tabtools
2.1.14.
}

\section{Differences from Stata}{

\itemize{
\item R has no frames: the returned table (and \code{\link[=as.data.frame]{as.data.frame()}} of it)
stands for \code{frame()}. No file is required.
\item Like \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} and \code{\link[=table1_tc]{table1_tc()}}, R prints no \verb{Exported to ...} or
\verb{Markdown exported to ...} lines; the listing is the printed table.
\item \code{level} also takes a proportion (\code{0.9}), as in \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}}.
\item An infinite \code{pyscale} or \code{ratescale} is refused (Stata has no
infinities; a missing one is refused on both sides).
\item Blocks with negative, infinite, or non-numeric counts, person-time, or
rates are refused, \code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} results and strate-shaped blocks alike;
Stata formats whatever the file holds.
\item \code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} blocks computed with \code{per} other than 1 need an explicit
\code{ratescale} (see Scaling and intervals).
\item Labels and identities are character vectors, one element each, rather
than one string split at \verb{\\}.
\item Blocks from \code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} of time and event data name their exposures
and outcomes by default (the grouping and event columns' labels, else
their names; see \code{explabels} and \code{outlabels}). Stata's \code{strate} files
carry no such names, so for them, as in Stata, the defaults are
\verb{Exposure 1}, ... and \verb{Outcome 1}, ....
}
}

\examples{
# Rates per 1,000 person-years from time and event data: one tt_rates()
# block per outcome, the arms as the categories
set.seed(2)
d <- data.frame(years = rexp(400, 0.05), event = rbinom(400, 1, 0.4),
                relapse = rbinom(400, 1, 0.2), arm = rep(c("A", "B"), 200),
                sex = rep(c("Men", "Women"), each = 200))
blocks <- list(tt_rates(d, "years", "event", by = "arm"),
               tt_rates(d, "years", "relapse", by = "arm"))
tab <- stratetab(blocks, outcomes = 2, outlabels = c("Death", "Relapse"),
                 outcomeids = c("death", "relapse"), explabels = "Arm")
tab

# Rate ratios compare each exposure group with the first, category by
# category: here women with men, within each arm
men <- d[d$sex == "Men", ]
women <- d[d$sex == "Women", ]
stratetab(list(tt_rates(men, "years", "event", by = "arm"),
               tt_rates(women, "years", "event", by = "arm")),
          outcomes = 1, outlabels = "Death", explabels = c("Men", "Women"),
          rateratio = TRUE)

# Written to a workbook, the table is returned invisibly
tab <- stratetab(blocks, outcomes = 2, xlsx = tempfile(fileext = ".xlsx"))

# Blocks saved by Stata's strate (read with haven::read_dta()) have
# _D, _Y, _Rate, _Lower and _Upper columns; the interval labels give
# the level
blk <- function(d, y, rate, lo, hi) {
  b <- data.frame(drug = c("SSRI", "SNRI"), `_D` = d, `_Y` = y, `_Rate` = rate,
                  `_Lower` = lo, `_Upper` = hi, check.names = FALSE)
  attr(b[["_Lower"]], "label") <- "Lower 95\% confidence limit"
  attr(b[["_Upper"]], "label") <- "Upper 95\% confidence limit"
  b
}
cv_m <- blk(c(178, 161), c(28100, 22300), c(0.00633, 0.00722),
            c(0.00545, 0.00616), c(0.00734, 0.00844))
cv_f <- blk(c(134, 128), c(24380, 19520), c(0.00550, 0.00656),
            c(0.00462, 0.00549), c(0.00652, 0.00781))
stratetab(list(cv_m, cv_f), outcomes = 1, outlabels = "CV events",
          explabels = c("Male", "Female"), rateratio = TRUE)
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} for the blocks; \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} for the hazard-ratio
models beside them.
}
